# =============================================================================
# Trivy scan exceptions — PINNED UPSTREAM IMAGES.  (policy: Phase 226 / 822)
# Consumed by `make scan-images` ONLY.
# =============================================================================
#
# SPLIT FROM `.trivyignore` IN PHASE 822 — see .trivyignore.first-party for the
# full rationale. Short version: one shared ignorefile meant a waiver for a
# monitoring sidecar silently covered the same CVE in our own proxy image.
#
# THE RULE HERE
#   An entry is valid only when **no fix is reachable without replacing the
#   image**. That is deliberately weaker than the first-party rule, and it is
#   the honest criterion: these are third-party tags we cannot rebuild, so a
#   fixed version existing upstream in Go or a Go module does NOT help us until
#   the image publisher ships a new tag.
#
#   The old single file's header claimed "only when there is genuinely no fix
#   available", which was never true of most entries here — 70 of 73 carried a
#   non-empty FixedVersion. Stating the real rule makes the list defensible
#   instead of quietly contradictory.
#
#   Before renewing, re-check for a newer upstream tag and compare raw CVE-ID
#   sets — not release notes. Several "newer" tags are measured net regressions
#   (cadvisor v0.54.1/v0.55.1, grafana 13.1.3); those findings are recorded
#   inline below.
#
#   Every entry needs: a '#' block stating why no fix AND why not exploitable
#   here, plus an inline `exp:YYYY-MM-DD` at most 7 days out.
#
#   Verify with `make scan-exceptions`. Guide:
#   docs/runbooks/security_scan_exceptions.md
# =============================================================================


# =============================================================================
# Trivy scan exceptions — JUSTIFIED, TIME-WINDOWED only.   (policy: Phase 226)
# =============================================================================
#
# HOW THIS WORKS
#   `make scan` passes each finding listed here to Trivy's --ignorefile, so a
#   listed CVE no longer fails the gate UNTIL its `exp:` date, after which Trivy
#   stops ignoring it and the scan fails again — forcing a re-review.
#   Run `make scan-exceptions` to list every entry with days-remaining.
#
# RULES
#   1. NO blanket ignores. One entry per CVE, only when there is genuinely no
#      fix available (anything fixable is patched via apt/apk upgrade instead).
#   2. Every entry MUST have a '#' comment block directly above it (WHY no fix +
#      WHY not exploitable here) and an inline expiry `CVE-XXXX exp:YYYY-MM-DD`.
#   3. Maximum window is 7 days (today + 7d) so exceptions never quietly
#      become permanent.
#
# OPERATOR: ADD / EDIT / DELETE
#   ADD    — append a '#' comment block then `CVE-... exp:<today+7d>`.
#   EDIT   — change the `exp:` date (re-justify in the comment) when extending.
#   DELETE — remove the comment block + CVE line once fixed or resolved.
#   Verify with `make scan-exceptions`. Full guide:
#   docs/runbooks/security_scan_exceptions.md
# =============================================================================
#
# ── 2026-08-06 remediation + renewal (Phase 818) ──
#   The 31 exceptions that expired 2026-08-06 were re-investigated for real
#   fixes (not just renewed). Outcome: NONE had an available fix.
#   • GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964 (msgpack) + CVE-2025-47273 (setuptools): both are
#     pip's OWN vendored copies (pip/_vendor), not dependencies we install;
#     unfixable by us even at the current pip 26.2.1. The build is
#     non-deterministic about which surfaces first, so both are exceptioned.
#   • The remaining 29 (grafana 13.0.4-ubuntu, promtail 3.6.11 EOL, alertmanager
#     v0.33.1, cadvisor v0.52.1, python-ecdsa, ja4proxy-test protobuf): confirmed
#     no better upstream tag exists (alertmanager/promtail are latest; grafana
#     13.1.x and cadvisor v0.55.1 carry a larger CVE set per the 2026-08-03
#     re-verification; ecdsa has no upstream patch).
#   All 31 renewed to today+7.
#   Recurrence fix: the Phase 812-B weekly renewal workflow (was broken — see
#   Phase 818 / issue #396) now keeps these fresh before they cliff.
#
# ── 2026-08-10 remediation + renewal (Phase 800) ──
#   42 exceptions had expired (exp:2026-08-10 — see the off-by-one note below).
#   Every affected image was re-scanned against its newest upstream tag with
#   `trivy image --severity HIGH,CRITICAL` and no ignorefile, comparing raw
#   CVE-ID sets rather than trusting release notes.
#   TWO REAL FIXES FOUND AND APPLIED (contra the 2026-08-06 conclusion):
#     • grafana/loki  3.7.4 -> 3.7.6          2 HIGH/CRITICAL -> 0, no regressions
#     • grafana/grafana 13.0.4 -> 13.0.6-ubuntu  9 -> 7, no regressions
#     Both clear CVE-2026-56852 + GHSA-hrxh-6v49-42gf *in those two images*.
#     The entries stay anyway: promtail 3.6.11, alertmanager v0.33.1,
#     haproxy-exporter v0.15.0 and cadvisor v0.52.1 still carry them and have
#     no newer tag at all (verified against Docker Hub / gcr.io tag lists).
#   CONFIRMED STILL UNFIXABLE (upgrade is a net regression — measured, not assumed):
#     • cadvisor v0.54.1 -> 44 and v0.55.1 -> 43 HIGH/CRITICAL vs v0.52.1's 42
#       (each clears 9-10 but introduces 11 new). Staying on v0.52.1.
#     • grafana 13.1.3-ubuntu -> 11 vs 13.0.6's 7 (clears 2, introduces 4).
#     • promtail / alertmanager / haproxy-exporter: already the newest tag
#       published; there is nothing to bump to.
#   All 72 renewed to 2026-08-17.
#
#   ⚠ WHY THESE EXPIRED UNNOTICED: `scripts/scan_exceptions.py` counted an entry
#   whose exp: date is TODAY as still valid, but trivy suppresses only while
#   `today < exp` — so the early-warning tool printed "✓ all within their time
#   window" and exited 0 on the same run where `make scan` failed on 42 of them.
#   Fixed 2026-08-10 (d <= 0 is now EXPIRED), with boundary tests.
#   The weekly Wed 05:00 UTC renewal cron also renewed to today+7, i.e. entries
#   expired at 00:00 on the very morning the job ran — a guaranteed red window
#   every week. Renewal now runs daily.

