# CVE-2024-41110: moby "Authz zero length regression" (CRITICAL) — a
# specially crafted API request with a spoofed Content-Length can bypass an
# authorization plugin gating the Docker daemon's API.
# Why no fix: transitively compiled into gcr.io/cadvisor/cadvisor:v0.52.1's
# github.com/docker/docker dependency. Re-verified 2026-08-03: v0.55.1 still
# carries this, plus a much larger CVE set overall — not an improvement.
# Why not exploitable: this bug lives in the Docker daemon's own
# authorization-plugin request handling. cAdvisor is a client of the Docker
# API, not a daemon serving one — it never accepts inbound Docker API
# requests that an authz plugin would need to gate.
CVE-2024-41110 exp:2026-08-23

# CVE-2025-31133 / CVE-2025-52565 / CVE-2025-52881: runc container-escape
# vulnerabilities (masked-path mount races, /dev/console mount races, and
# arbitrary procfs write-gadgets via opencontainers/selinux).
# Why no fix: gcr.io/cadvisor/cadvisor:v0.52.1 imports github.com/opencontainers/runc
# as a Go library dependency (container/OCI state helpers) — re-verified
# 2026-08-03, checked v0.55.1, which still carries this plus a much larger
# CVE set overall.
# Why not exploitable: these CVEs require runc's own container-creation code
# path to execute — the mount-namespace setup and masked-path/device
# handling that happens when a container is actually started. cAdvisor never
# creates or starts containers (it has no engine/runtime role); it only
# reads existing container stats via the Docker API and /sys, /proc. The
# vulnerable runc code is statically linked but never invoked.
CVE-2025-31133 exp:2026-08-23
CVE-2025-52565 exp:2026-08-23
CVE-2025-52881 exp:2026-08-23

